The original scans every message entry on each lookup. This version builds a 64K-entry index table once, on the first call, so each later lookup is a single array read.

- **Same results:** when two entries share a `num`, the table keeps the first one, as the linear scan did. Unknown numbers still return `NULL`.
- **Fallback:** if `MESGS` were ever 65535 or more, the indices would not fit, so the function keeps the linear scan.
- **Startup safety:** the table is a function-local static, so C++ guarantees it is built safely the first time. It is derived only from the constant `mesgs` array, so it does not depend on any mutable global state.
- **Memory:** the table costs 128 KB.

```cpp
const Profile::MESG* Profile::GetMesg(const FIT_UINT16 num)
{
    struct Index
    {
        FIT_UINT16 idx[65536];
        Index()
        {
            for (int k = 0; k < 65536; k++)
                idx[k] = 0xFFFF;
            for (int i = 0; i < MESGS; i++)
            {
                FIT_UINT16 n = mesgs[i].num;
                if (idx[n] == 0xFFFF)
                    idx[n] = (FIT_UINT16)i;
            }
        }
    };

    if (MESGS >= 0xFFFF)
    {
        for (int i = 0; i < MESGS; i++)
        {
            if (mesgs[i].num == num)
                return &mesgs[i];
        }
        return NULL;
    }

    static const Index index;
    FIT_UINT16 i = index.idx[num];
    if (i == 0xFFFF)
        return NULL;
    return &mesgs[i];
}
```